@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex introduction
@cindex memory store, shared
@cindex pipelines, multi-model
@cindex verbatim memory

Engram is a shared verbatim chat memory store for multi-model LLM
pipelines.  It keeps decisions, facts, and design rationale in a single
SQLite file --- with FTS5 full-text search --- that multiple models and
agents can read from and write to.  Memory persists across the stages
of a pipeline without requiring LLM calls to encode or retrieve
context: what was stored is returned verbatim.

@cindex SQLite
@cindex FTS5
@cindex full-text search

@section The three surfaces

@cindex surfaces, the three
Engram exposes one store through three surfaces.  All three dispatch to
the same underlying storage, so a memory written on any surface is
immediately visible to the other two.

@table @asis
@item CLI
@cindex CLI surface
The @command{engram} command-line tool, for shell scripts, hooks, and
direct use.  @xref{Invocation}.

@item MCP
@cindex MCP surface
A Model Context Protocol server over stdio (@command{engram mcp}), for
Claude Code, Codex, and other MCP clients.  @xref{MCP Server}.

@item HTTP
@cindex HTTP surface
A local-only HTTP API (@command{engram serve}), for any HTTP client:
@command{curl}, Kimi, Ollama Cloud, and so on.  @xref{HTTP API}.
@end table

@section Memories and rules

@cindex memories versus rules
Engram stores two kinds of durable state.  A @dfn{memory} records what
happened --- a decision, a fact, a piece of rationale --- scoped to a
project, task, or run identifier.  A @dfn{rule} states what must keep
being true: durable project policy that outlives any one session.
Rules are covered in their own chapter (@pxref{Rules}).

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@cindex invocation
@cindex command line

The general form is:

@example
engram [@var{global-options}] @var{command} [@var{command-options}] [@var{args}]
@end example

@section Global options

@cindex global options
@cindex options, global

@table @code
@item --db @var{path}
@cindex database path
Path to the shared SQLite database.  Defaults to @file{engram.db}; the
@env{ENGRAM_DB} environment variable overrides the default.  All agents
should point at the same file (or the same @command{engram serve}
instance) to actually share memory.

@item --json
@cindex JSON output
Alias for @code{--format json}.

@item --format @var{format}
@cindex output formats
Machine output format: @code{json}, @code{jsonl}, or @code{csv}.
Overrides mode auto-detection.  (@code{yaml} is deferred.)

@item --no-color
@cindex colors, disabling
Disable colored output.  The @env{NO_COLOR} environment variable has
the same effect.

@item --accessible
@cindex accessible output
@cindex accessibility
Accessible output (Standard @U{00A7}18): plain linear text, no color,
status tags.  Also enabled by @env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}=1;
@env{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}=0 disables auto-detection, but this flag still
wins.
@end table

@section Output modes

@cindex output modes
@cindex machine mode
@cindex mode cascade

Engram is a dual-mode CLI: human-readable text on a terminal, machine
output everywhere else.  The mode cascade, from strongest to weakest:

@enumerate
@item
An explicit @code{--format} or @code{--json} flag always wins.

@item
Otherwise, machine (JSON) mode is selected when @env{AI_AGENT} or
@env{AGENT} is set non-empty, or when @env{CI} is truthy.

@item
Otherwise, machine (JSON) mode is selected when stdout is not a TTY.
@end enumerate

Human mode therefore requires a real TTY on stdout.

@subsection The metadata envelope

@cindex envelope, metadata
@cindex metadata envelope
Every machine-mode response is wrapped in an envelope:

@verbatim
{"metadata":{"tool":"engram","version":"0.2.0","command":"recall",
             "timestamp":"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z"},
 "data":...}
@end verbatim

The @code{metadata} object carries @code{tool}, @code{version},
@code{command}, and an ISO 8601 UTC @code{timestamp}; it optionally
carries @code{tool_agent} (when an agent environment was detected),
@code{dry_run} (on dry runs), and @code{budget} (when token budgeting
was requested --- see @command{engram recall} below).  The payload is
under @code{data}.

@subsection JSON Lines output

@cindex JSONL
@cindex JSON Lines
With @code{--format jsonl}, the first line is the metadata envelope
with @code{"data": null}; then, for array results, one line follows per
record --- or, for a single object, one line with that object.

@subsection CSV output

@cindex CSV
@cindex RFC 4180
With @code{--format csv}, the data records are emitted as RFC 4180 CSV
rows on stdout, with the header row derived from the first record's
keys.  The metadata envelope goes to stderr as one JSON line.

@subsection Errors and exit codes

@cindex errors, structured
@cindex exit codes
In machine mode, errors are a single line of JSON on stderr:

@verbatim
{"error":{"code":"...","exit_code":2,"message":"...","hint":"...",
          "timestamp":"...","command":"...","docs_url":"..."}}
@end verbatim

Exit codes:

@table @code
@item 0
Success.
@item 1
Internal or storage error.
@item 2
Invalid argument.
@item 3
Not found.
@item 5
Conflict --- the supersede target was already superseded.
@end table

@section engram remember

@cindex remember command
@cindex storing a memory

@example
engram remember --agent @var{name} --scope @var{id} [--role @var{role}] [--supersedes @var{id}] [--dry-run] [@var{content}]
@end example

Store a verbatim message.  When @var{content} is omitted, it is read
from stdin.  @code{--role} defaults to @code{note} (alternatives:
@code{user}, @code{assistant}, @code{system}).

@cindex dry run
With @code{--dry-run}, the message is validated and the would-be record
shown, without writing anything; the machine envelope carries
@code{"dry_run": true}.

@cindex supersession
@cindex bi-temporal validity
With @code{--supersedes @var{id}}, the new memory replaces an earlier
one in the @emph{same scope}: in one transaction the target's validity
window is closed (@code{valid_to} set to the new record's timestamp,
@code{superseded_by} set to its id) and the replacement is stored.
Nothing is ever deleted --- reads simply default to currently-valid
rows.  Outcomes: an unknown or cross-scope target exits 3; a rule
target exits 2 (rules have their own lifecycle); an already-superseded
target exits 5 with a @code{CONFLICT} error naming the winner.

@section engram recall

@cindex recall command

@example
engram recall --scope @var{id} [--limit @var{n}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}] [--as-of @var{instant} | --include-superseded]
@end example

Read back the last @var{n} messages for a scope, in chronological
order.  @code{--limit} defaults to 50.

@cindex time travel
@cindex as-of queries
By default only currently-valid memories are returned (superseded rows
are hidden).  @code{--as-of @var{instant}} (ISO 8601 UTC, e.g.@:
@code{2026-08-01T12:00:00Z}) time-travels to the memories that were
valid at that instant; @code{--include-superseded} returns the full
verbatim history.  The two are mutually exclusive.  @command{engram
search} accepts the same pair.

@cindex budget, token
@cindex token budget
With @code{--budget-tokens}, the results are packed to a token budget:
the newest messages have priority, so when the budget bites it is the
@emph{oldest} that are dropped, and the surviving messages are still
returned in chronological order.  Token costs are estimated as
@code{ceil(characters / 4)} (minimum 1) --- a multi-model pipeline has
no single correct tokenizer, so Engram uses this crude heuristic and
names it in the response: the envelope gains @code{metadata.budget}
with @code{estimator} (@code{"chars-div-4"}),
@code{requested_tokens}, @code{estimated_tokens} (included items
only), @code{included}, @code{dropped}, @code{dropped_ids}, and
per-channel candidate counts under @code{channels}.  Without the flag,
the output is byte-identical to earlier releases.

@section engram search

@cindex search command

@example
engram search @var{query} [--scope @var{id}] [--limit @var{n}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}]
@end example

Full-text (FTS5) search across stored memories, optionally restricted
to one scope.  @code{--limit} defaults to 20.  Query tokens are
sanitized --- each is matched as a quoted phrase --- so arbitrary input
cannot inject FTS5 syntax.

With @code{--budget-tokens}, the ranked results are packed to a token
budget in rank order --- relevance ordering is the point of a search
result, so it is both the packing priority and the output order.  An
over-budget hit is dropped and later, smaller hits are still tried.
The envelope reports the same @code{metadata.budget} object as
@command{engram recall}.

@section engram context

@cindex context command
@cindex session start
@cindex reciprocal rank fusion

@example
engram context [--scope @var{id}] [--query @var{q}] [--budget-tokens @var{n}] [--limit @var{n}]
@end example

Assemble a budget-packed context block for session start: the scope's
active rules first, then memories fused from recency and relevance.
@code{--budget-tokens} defaults to 3000; @code{--limit} (candidates
fetched per channel) defaults to 50.  @code{--scope} resolves through
the same cascade as the @command{rule} commands (@pxref{Rules}).

The rules are @emph{always all included}, even when they alone exceed
the budget --- policy is never silently dropped; the overrun is
visible in the budget report.  Memories are then packed into whatever
budget remains.

Without @code{--query}, memories are selected newest-first.  With
@code{--query}, the recency channel (newest first) and the full-text
relevance channel (rank order) are fused with reciprocal rank fusion
(@code{k = 60}), so an old-but-relevant memory can displace a
new-but-irrelevant one.  Selection order and presentation order
differ by design: whichever way a memory earned its slot, the
included memories are presented chronologically, which is how a
prompt reads best.  Rule rows surfaced by recall or search are
skipped as candidates --- they already lead the block.

The data carries @code{rules}, @code{memories}, @code{rules_tokens},
and the @code{budget} report; the envelope repeats the report as
@code{metadata.budget}.

@section engram rule

@cindex rule command

Durable project rules --- policy that outlives a session
(@pxref{Rules} for the concept and invariants).

@subsection rule add

@example
engram rule add --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}] [--agent @var{name}] [@var{text}]
@end example

Record a rule, or revise the existing rule with the same @code{--id}
(an upsert --- never a second, competing copy).  @var{text} is read
from stdin when omitted.  @code{--agent} defaults to
@env{ENGRAM_AGENT}, else @code{engram-cli}.  Storing a rule does not
surface it to anyone --- follow with @command{engram rule sync}.

@subsection rule list

@example
engram rule list [--scope @var{id}] [--include-retired]
@end example

List the rules in effect for a scope, ordered by id.
@code{--include-retired} also shows withdrawn rules, flagged with
@code{"retired": true}.

@subsection rule retire

@example
engram rule retire --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}]
@end example

Withdraw a rule so it stops being binding.  The rule is tombstoned, not
deleted (@pxref{Rules}).  Retiring an unknown id is an error (exit
code 3).

@subsection rule purge

@cindex purge, rule
@cindex deleting a rule

@example
engram rule purge --id @var{kebab-id} [--scope @var{id}] --yes [--dry-run]
@end example

Permanently delete a @emph{retired} rule's row --- the one true delete
in Engram, and deliberately CLI-only: it exists on neither the MCP nor
the HTTP surface, because destructive operations are not
agent-invocable.  An active rule is refused (exit 2; retire it first),
an unknown id exits 3, and without @code{--yes} nothing is deleted
(the process may not have a TTY to ask on, so consent must be
explicit).  @code{--dry-run} previews the deletion.

@subsection rule sync

@example
engram rule sync [--scope @var{id}] [--file @var{path}]@dots{} [--dry-run]
@end example

Render the scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md} and @file{CLAUDE.md}
(or the given @code{--file} targets, repeatable).  Only the region
between the engram sentinels is rewritten.  With @code{--dry-run},
reports what would be written without touching any file.

@section engram mcp

@cindex mcp command

@example
engram mcp [--db @var{path}]
@end example

Run as an MCP server over stdio.  @xref{MCP Server}.

@section engram serve

@cindex serve command

@example
engram serve [--addr @var{ip:port}] [--db @var{path}]
@end example

Run the HTTP API.  @code{--addr} defaults to @code{127.0.0.1:8420}.
@xref{HTTP API}.

@section engram schema

@cindex schema command
@cindex introspection

@example
engram schema
@end example

Print the JSON Schema for Engram's data types, as
@code{@{"Memory": @dots{}, "Rule": @dots{}@}} (CLI Standard
introspection).

@section engram describe

@cindex describe command
@cindex capability manifest

@example
engram describe
@end example

Print the machine-readable CLI Standard capability manifest (JSON).

@section engram save-chat

@cindex save-chat command
@cindex chat history, exporting

@example
engram save-chat --scope @var{id} [--file @var{path}] [--model @var{name}]
@end example

Save a complete chat history for a scope to a Texinfo file.
@code{--file} defaults to @file{chat/@var{timestamp}.texi};
@code{--model} records an optional signature model/agent name (for
example @code{gpt-5.6-pro}).

@node MCP Server
@chapter MCP Server

@cindex MCP server
@cindex Model Context Protocol
@cindex stdio transport

@command{engram mcp} runs a Model Context Protocol server over stdio,
suitable for wiring into Claude Code, Codex, and other MCP clients:

@example
engram mcp --db /path/to/engram.db
@end example

The server exposes nine tools.  The memory tools mirror the CLI
commands of the same names; the rule tools mirror @command{engram
rule}.  (The tool surface is deliberately narrow: every tool's schema
costs context on every turn of every conversation, so Engram budgets a
hard ceiling of ten.)

@table @code
@item remember
@cindex remember tool (MCP)
Store a verbatim chat message in shared memory, scoped to a
project/task/run id.  @code{role} defaults to @code{note}.

@item recall
@cindex recall tool (MCP)
Read back the last N memories for a scope, in chronological order
(default limit 50).

@item search
@cindex search tool (MCP)
Full-text search across stored memories, optionally restricted to one
scope (default limit 20).

@item get
@cindex get tool (MCP)
Fetch a single memory by id --- the drill-down half of search/recall.
Looks across the full history, superseded rows included: an id lookup
answers ``what is this row'', not ``what is true now''.

@item context
@cindex context tool (MCP)
Assemble a budget-packed session-start block: the scope's active rules
first (always included), then recency+relevance fused memories packed
to the token budget (default 3000).  One call replaces separate
@code{recall} + @code{rule_list} at session start.

@item rule_add
@cindex rule_add tool (MCP)
Record a durable project rule, or revise the existing rule with the
same @code{rule_id}.  @code{agent} defaults to @code{mcp-client}.

@item rule_list
@cindex rule_list tool (MCP)
List the durable rules in effect for a scope, ordered by
@code{rule_id}.  Call at session start to load standing policy.

@item rule_retire
@cindex rule_retire tool (MCP)
Withdraw a rule so it stops being binding.  Tombstones rather than
deletes.

@item rule_sync
@cindex rule_sync tool (MCP)
Render a scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md} and @file{CLAUDE.md} at
the project root.
@end table

@cindex scope, under MCP
One caution: the rule tools' scope cascade (@pxref{Rules}) resolves
against the @emph{server process's} working directory, not the
client's.  A shared server therefore needs an explicit @code{scope}
argument on every rule call.

@node HTTP API
@chapter HTTP API

@cindex HTTP API
@cindex routes

@command{engram serve} binds a local-only HTTP API --- default
@code{127.0.0.1:8420}, no authentication.  Do not expose it beyond the
loopback interface.

@cindex status codes
As of 0.2.0, @emph{all} routes return real HTTP status codes (a
deliberate breaking change: before 0.2.0 the @code{/v1/memory*}
handlers answered @code{200 OK} with an error body).  Success
responses carry the same metadata envelope as the CLI's machine mode.

@table @code
@item POST /v1/memory
@cindex POST /v1/memory
Store a memory.  Body: @code{@{"agent": @dots{}, "scope": @dots{},
"content": @dots{}@}} (optional @code{role}, optional
@code{supersedes}).  Returns @code{400} if @code{content} is empty or
whitespace-only, @code{200} with the stored Memory in the envelope on
success, @code{500} on a storage error.  With @code{supersedes}: the
target's validity window is closed in the same transaction; @code{404}
for an unknown or cross-scope target, @code{400} for a rule target,
and @code{409 Conflict} when the target was already superseded (the
message names the winning id).

@item GET /v1/memory/:id
@cindex GET /v1/memory/:id
Drill-down by id, across the full history (superseded rows included).
@code{404} when no such id exists.

@item GET /v1/memory/recall?scope=&limit=&budget_tokens=&as_of=&include_superseded=
@cindex GET /v1/memory/recall
Fetch the last N memories for a scope, chronological order.  Reads
default to currently-valid rows; @code{as_of} (ISO 8601 UTC)
time-travels and @code{include_superseded=true} shows the full history
(@code{400} if the pair is combined or the instant is malformed ---
@code{search} accepts the same parameters).  With
@code{budget_tokens}, results are packed to the token budget
(newest-first priority, chronological output) and the envelope carries
@code{metadata.budget} --- the same behavior as
@code{engram recall --budget-tokens} (@pxref{Invocation}).

@item GET /v1/memory/search?query=&scope=&limit=
@cindex GET /v1/memory/search
Full-text search, optionally scoped.  @code{budget_tokens} packs the
ranked results to a token budget, in rank order.

@item GET /v1/context?scope=&query=&budget_tokens=&limit=
@cindex GET /v1/context
A budget-packed context block for session start: active rules first
(always all included, even over budget), then recency+relevance fused
memories --- the same data shape as @command{engram context}
(@pxref{Invocation}).  @code{scope} resolves through the rule-command
cascade against the server process's working directory when omitted;
@code{budget_tokens} defaults to 3000 and @code{limit} to 50.  An
unknown scope yields @code{200} with empty rules and memories, not an
error.  Returns @code{500} on a storage failure.

@item POST /v1/rules
@cindex POST /v1/rules
Create or revise a rule (re-using a @code{rule_id} updates in place).
Returns @code{400} for an invalid id or text.

@item GET /v1/rules
@cindex GET /v1/rules
Active rules for a scope (@code{?scope=}); @code{?include_retired=}
adds tombstoned rules.

@item DELETE /v1/rules/:rule_id
@cindex DELETE /v1/rules/:rule_id
Retire (tombstone --- soft delete) a rule.  Returns @code{404} for an
unknown rule, @code{200} with the outcome on success.

@item POST /v1/rules/sync
@cindex POST /v1/rules/sync
Render the scope's rules into @file{AGENTS.md} and @file{CLAUDE.md}.
This is the only route that writes outside the database; targets
derive from the server process's working directory, never from caller
input.

@item GET /v1/health
@cindex GET /v1/health
Liveness probe.
@end table

@node Environment Variables
@chapter Environment Variables

@cindex environment variables

@table @env
@item ENGRAM_DB
@cindex ENGRAM_DB
Database file path; overrides the @file{engram.db} default (the
@code{--db} flag overrides both).

@item ENGRAM_SCOPE
@cindex ENGRAM_SCOPE
Default scope for the @command{rule} commands, consulted by the scope
cascade (@pxref{Rules}).

@item ENGRAM_AGENT
@cindex ENGRAM_AGENT
Default @code{--agent} for @command{rule add} (falling back to
@code{engram-cli}).

@item AI_AGENT
@itemx AGENT
@cindex AI_AGENT
@cindex AGENT
Presence-based agent detection: set to any non-empty value, they
select machine output mode (and record @code{tool_agent} in the
envelope).

@item CI
@cindex CI
Truthy-based: selects machine output mode when set to a non-empty
value other than @code{0} or @code{false} (case-insensitive).

@item NO_COLOR
@cindex NO_COLOR
Set non-empty, disables colored output (same as @code{--no-color}).

@item SPACECRAFT_A11Y
@cindex SPACECRAFT_A11Y
Accessible output toggle (Standard @U{00A7}18): @code{0} is an explicit
off; any other non-empty value enables accessible mode.  The
@code{--accessible} flag always wins.
@end table

@node Rules
@chapter Rules

@cindex rules, durable
@cindex policy, durable

A memory records what happened; a @dfn{rule} states what must keep
being true.  Rules are durable project policy: stored once, revised in
place, and rendered into the markdown files agent harnesses load
automatically, so the policy keeps applying without anyone remembering
to restate it.  Rules are available on all three surfaces.

@section Invariants

@cindex invariants, rules
Four invariants worth not breaking:

@enumerate
@item
@cindex sync, as delivery
@emph{Sync is the delivery mechanism, not an export.}  A row in SQLite
never reaches a model's context.  Rendering into @file{AGENTS.md} and
@file{CLAUDE.md} --- files harnesses auto-load --- is what makes a rule
take effect.

@item
@emph{The rendered block is a pure function of the rules.}  No
generation timestamp; rules ordered by @code{rule_id}.  That makes
sync idempotent (unchanged rules mean no write) and therefore safe in
a hook or commit gate.

@item
@emph{Add upserts.}  Re-using a @code{rule_id} revises in place;
@code{created_at} survives, @code{updated_at} moves.  Two competing
copies of a rule are worse than none.

@item
@cindex tombstone
@emph{Retire tombstones, never deletes.}  A retired rule disappears
from listings and synced files but stays in the database and remains
searchable --- ``we used to require this'' is worth keeping.
Re-running @command{rule add} with the same id reinstates it.
Retiring is idempotent; an unknown id is an error (exit code 3 on the
CLI, @code{404} over HTTP), not a silent success.
@end enumerate

@section Sentinels

@cindex sentinels
@cindex managed block
The rendered block sits between HTML-comment sentinels; only that
region is ever rewritten, and everything outside it is preserved
verbatim:

@verbatim
<!-- engram:rules:begin ... -->
   ... rendered rules ...
<!-- engram:rules:end -->
@end verbatim

Rule text containing @code{engram:rules:} is rejected at write time
--- it would terminate its own block.  An opening sentinel with no
closing one is treated as a mangled block and replaced wholesale.

@section Scope cascade

@cindex scope cascade
@cindex scope_origin
When @code{--scope} is not given, the scope resolves through a
cascade, and the chosen origin is reported as @code{scope_origin}:

@enumerate
@item
The explicit @code{--scope} argument.
@item
The @env{ENGRAM_SCOPE} environment variable.
@item
The git working-tree basename.
@item
The current directory basename.
@end enumerate

Under MCP this cascade resolves against the server process's working
directory, so a shared server needs an explicit scope
(@pxref{MCP Server}).

